{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_971141370#2_1271475398","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage quotes the definition of retail therapy as shopping to improve the buyer's mood and mentions comfort buys during depression or transition, which supports a mood-buying link. However, the document appears to be a language/word-choice answer rather than a source dedicated to the psychological or consumer-behavior question.","learned":"Retail therapy is commonly used to describe purchases made to improve mood, especially during depression or transitions."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2961","reason":"The retail-therapy search is producing direct mood-shopping evidence, so continuing may uncover more dedicated articles or studies worthy of grade 3."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2961"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_838426896#0_1558567833","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2961","rank":3,"snippet":"Shopping Is Actually Good for You | TIME.com Psychology of Money Is Retail Therapy for Real? 5 Ways Shopping Is Actually Good for You By Kit Yarrow April 16, 2013 Share Read Later Send to Kindle Daly and Newton / Getty Images You know the phrase, “When the going gets tough, the tough go shopping”? There just may be some wisdom in that. A survey conducted by TNS Global on behalf of Ebates.com found that more than half of Americans (52%, including 64% of women and 40% of men) admit to engaging in “<cover>retail therapy”—the act of shopping and spending to improve one’s mood. </cover>This echoes a previous study, published in the Journal of Psychology and Marketing, that revealed 62% of shoppers had purchased something to cheer themselves up, and another 28% had purchased as a form of celebration. But beyond the quick rush provided by making a purchase, is “retail therapy” actually therapeutic? Renowned San Francisco therapist Peggy Wynne, who is known to personally appreciate the mood-boosting quality of a great pair of shoes, says that it can be. “ We all enjoy a little retail therapy now and then,” she told me. “ In small, manageable doses "}}
