{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ mood shopping spending)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_855508554#8_1753932240","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2965","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_855508554#8_1753932240\" , \"end_char\": 3934.000000 , \"headings\": \"Bipolar excessive spending Bipolar excessive spending TABLE OF CONTENTS Bipolar excessive spending vs compulsive shopping Precautions Bipolar treatment contracts The morning after Quotes on bipolar spending sprees Some comments by Carolina Estevez, Psy.D. Join the Bipolar Community ! \" , \"segment\": \"Bipolar excessive <cover>spending vs compulsive shopping Until that fateful day when my psychiatrist started to help me understand my mood </cover>disorder, I had known I had a problem with money. Fortunately I was a high earner and so could get away with more. Basically I thought that I was just another shopaholic. So how do we tell the difference between manic or Bipolar spending versus “ordinary” compulsive spending? This question was addressed in a research study by John M. Kuzma, MD and Donald W. Black, MD, “Compulsive shopping: When spending begins to consume the consumer”: “ Bipolar mania and excessive spending related to a compulsive buying disorder are relatively easy to differentiate: The manic patient’s unrestrained spending sprees correspond to manic episodes and are "}}
