{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1021036579#16_2047906352","relevance":1,"reason":"The page is an emoji-meaning guide and only casually says a money-face emoji can indicate being in the mood for shopping or spending. It is not evidence about whether mood affects buying behavior.","learned":"Some results use 'mood' and 'shopping/spending' colloquially without providing substantive evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"emotional spending\" mood shopping)","reason":"Search for the specific concept of emotional spending, which should retrieve documents directly explaining how moods and emotions influence purchases or spending."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"emotional spending\" mood shopping)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_756480308#10_1554028577","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2966","rank":1,"snippet":"\"segment\": \"1 Before you grab that last roll, stop and ask yourself if you really need it—or if you’ll be good with the closetful you have at home! 5. Spending to Feel Better Forty-five percent of Americans say they’re spending more during the pandemic. 2 One study found that nearly three out of four people said buying something impulsively during the pandemic has positively affected their <cover>mood. 3 This is called emotional spending, and we’ve probably all been guilty of it at one point or another this year. I mean, the future is kind of up in the air, and it feels good to just dull the stress and spark some joy with a little shopping, </cover>right? But that hit of happiness won’t last! If you want to feel at peace, stay focused on your goals without letting feelings, people or a pandemic get in the way! How to Break Bad Spending Habits Making a few good decisions over and over will set you up for long-term success. So now that you’re aware of which spending habits have got to go, here’s what to implement instead.\""}}
