{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ sadness spending buying)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_21_578097819#6_1220599678","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2967","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_21_578097819#6_1220599678\" , \"end_char\": 4070.000000 , \"headings\": \"How Does a Severe Bipolar Parent Affect Children? How Does a Severe Bipolar Parent Affect Children? Poor Judgment Aggressive or Risky Behavior Spending<cover>Sadness Loss of Interest \" , \"segment\": \"Many times they spend money they do not have. Spending money plays a huge part in the life of a manic bipolar. Some bipolar parents get a high off of buying </cover>their kids new toys or outfits, only to later verbally put down their behavior by saying things such as “Why did I spend so much? Sadness Difficulties of Living With a Bipolar Partner Learn More On the opposite end of the manic spectrum lies depression and sadness for many bipolar parents. The sadness can lead to hours and days of constant depression. This sadness becomes apparent to the children of the family. Seeing their parent crying and emotionally distraught can be heartbreaking to a child because he feels helpless, or he may blame himself. On the opposite end of the manic spectrum lies depression "}}
