{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1197603862#6_2602725444","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is about spending behavior and directly has a mood section stating that different moods and emotional states can make people more prone to impulse shopping, that upset/stressed/anxious people may seek retail therapy to feel better, and that people should identify moods that affect spending behavior.","learned":"Moods and emotional states can alter impulse-shopping risk; stress, anxiety, or upset feelings may lead people to shop for retail therapy, while other moods can make deals seem more attractive."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2973","reason":"This query produced a direct grade-3 result about mood affecting shopping/spending, so continuing may find more documents explicitly explaining shopping to feel better or mood-driven purchases."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2973"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1197603862#5_2602723280","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2973","rank":2,"snippet":"of times when you’re more likely to spend impulsively. So, take away the temptation by either steering clear of such environments, or only taking a few dollars with you. Likewise, if you have a favorite store and you find yourself wandering through the aisles looking for great deals, do all you can to limit your opportunities to go there. If going to your favorite store is unavoidable, keep your money – and credit cards -- safe from yourself (see more about this later). <cover>Mood Different moods and emotional states can alter our energy resources, making us more prone to impulse shopping. For example, if we’re upset, stressed or anxious we may seek some retail therapy to feel better. </cover>But instead of hitting the mall or your favorite internet shopping site, hit the gym or the park. Going for a walk or doing some exercise will do wonders for lifting up your mood.\" , \"start_char\": 1895.000000 , \"title\": \"How to Stop Spending Money: 7 Tips and Tricks to Curb Your Overspending | My Money Coach\" , \"url\": \"https://www.mymoneycoach.ca/blog/how-to-stop-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"37/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1197603862#5_2602723280","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a near-duplicate segment of the same My Money Coach page, but it directly states that different moods and emotional states can make people more prone to impulse shopping and that upset, stressed, or anxious people may seek retail therapy to feel better.","learned":"Mood can affect spending behavior by increasing susceptibility to impulse shopping, especially when people feel upset, stressed, or anxious."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ impulse shopping mood)","reason":"Search specifically for impulse shopping and mood, a focused direction likely to produce additional direct evidence on mood-driven buying without relying only on retail-therapy pages."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ impulse shopping mood)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_570818059#2_1349215497","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2974","rank":3,"snippet":"addiction, it can lead to professional, marital, and family problems. Although there is some debate about whether this condition is indeed a mental disorder, compulsive shopping is listed as an “impulse control disorder” by the World Health Organization in its International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems (ICD). 2 ﻿ Key Takeaways Compulsive shopping is an addiction to shopping for gratification that can lead to severe psychological and emotional issues. Compulsive shopping is generally exhibited by insecure people with low self-esteem and low impulse control. It can also be a symptom of serious mental ailments, such as bipolar disorder. Therapy and antidepressant medication can help in the treatment of compulsive shopping. Diagnosing Compulsive Shopping Compulsive shoppers typically are insecure people with low self-esteem and low <cover>impulse control. Not surprisingly, people with mood, anxiety, and eating disorders often exhibit symptoms. Sometimes compulsive shopping </cover>can also manifest itself as part of a much more severe illness, such as obsessive-compulsive disorder.\" , \"start_char\": 1395.000000 , \"title\": \"Compulsive Shopping Definition\" , \"url\": \"https://www.investopedia.com/terms/c/compulsive-shopping.asp\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1197603862#6_2602725444","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1197603862#5_2602723280","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
