{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"emotional spending\" buying mood)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_756480308#9_1554026911","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2982","rank":1,"snippet":"\": \"4. Making Impulse Purchases You probably know impulse buys are bad news for your bank account. In fact, Americans spend over $2,100 on impulse buys every year! We often impulse-buy items like food, clothing and even cars. But during the 2020 pandemic, the top impulse-buy items have been cleaning supplies, hand sanitizer and, of course, toilet paper. 1 Before you grab that last roll, stop and ask yourself if you really need it—or if you’ll be good with the closetful you have at home! 5. Spending to Feel Better Forty-five percent of Americans say they’re spending more during the pandemic. 2 One study found that nearly three out of four people said <cover>buying something impulsively during the pandemic has positively affected their mood. 3 This is called emotional spending, </cover>and we’ve probably all been guilty of it at one point or another this year.\" , \"start_char\": 3959.000000 , \"title\": \"How to Change Your Spending Habits | RamseySolutions.com\" , \"url\": \"https://www.daveramsey.com/blog/money-habits-we-need-to-break\" }"}}
